{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>And it came to pass in the evening, etc.</b> Great anguish was caused by the deception of the righteous man, and it caused a scattering of the power of holiness. Go forth and learn (Zohar Vayishlach 176a) that this is the reason the holiness of the firstborn was uprooted — in which kingdom and priesthood would be gathered, and a double portion in the inheritance. And from the aspect of the deed: that Jacob intended Rachel but performed his act with Leah, the aspects of holiness were scattered and the strength of the holy was weakened." "<b>And he took Leah.</b> Perhaps she did not wish to deceive Jacob, and he took her against her will or by persuasion of words; and other than this, there would have been no need to say anything other than ״וַיָּבֵא לֵאָה בִתּוֹ אֵלָיו״ (and he brought Leah his daughter to him)." "<b>And he brought her.</b> He was cunning to bring her with his own hand, and he did not entrust her neither to the messengers of the father nor to the messengers of the groom, so that he would not have time to recognize her beforehand. And he brought her with his own hand, and it is not customary for the groom to prepare himself before her, and Laban stood there until close to the time of his entry to her, to minimize for him the time of recognition." "<b>And he came to her.</b> The reason he did not perceive her was because of the scrupulousness of a righteous, upright, and faithful-spirited man, lest he stumble in the human snare — that desire might crave before coming near her, and defiling drippings of impurity flow from it before the commencement of a mitzvah. And who is greater for us than Jacob our father? The Torah bears witness concerning him (Genesis 49:3) ״רֵאשִׁית אוֹנִי״, and see what we have written there. Therefore he kept from looking at her, and immediately and at once came to her, to guard his fervor lest he profane his covenant, a holy covenant. And even after completing the act of relations, he did not recognize her, for she had extinguished the lights as is the practice for those who love His name (Orach Chaim 240) until the light of morning."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 29, :notes {3 "The parenthetical reference אורח חיים רמ cites Shulchan Aruch Orach Chaim 240, the chapter dealing with laws of conduct on the marital union and the practice of intimacy in darkness."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:44:56.241653479Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}